{
  "dom_id": "step-q42959",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}|\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|A|=0.$\n\n- Since $B$ is a diagonal matrix, we obtain \n\n$$\n|B|=3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5=-15\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has identical columns: \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$",
